// `@`, `$$i`, `$$m`, `move` and an assignment target all need a PLACE.
//
// A place is a name, a field or element of one, or what a pointer points at. The
// language has no way to spell the address of a temporary, so an expression that
// produces a value and keeps it nowhere has no address to take.
//
// NOTHING IN THE REAL FRONTEND ASKED THIS UNTIL CYCLE 0.5.3. The SEED refuses it —
// it has to emit an address and there is none — so the two disagreed about what a
// valid program is, which is the same situation `fixed` was in before 0.5.2. Four
// of the five sites below were unchecked entirely; the fifth looked only at the
// outermost operator and accepted `f().x = 5i32;`.
//
// These are TYPE errors and not analysis errors, and that is why they are in a file
// of their own: the static analyses run only over a program the type checker
// accepted, so a `NITPICK-TYPE-024` here would stop `moves.npk` before its own
// rules ever ran.

mod:places;
struct:Box = { int32:v; };

func:make = Box() never fails { pass Box{ v: 1i32 }; };

// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-024
func:address_of_a_temporary = NIL() never fails {
    int32->:p = @(raw make()).v;
    discard(p);
    pass NIL;
};

// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-024
func:borrow_of_a_temporary = NIL() never fails {
    int32->:q = $$m (raw make()).v;
    discard(q);
    pass NIL;
};

// A CALL'S RESULT HAS NO ADDRESS, so there is nothing to move out of.
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-024
func:move_a_temporary = NIL() never fails {
    Box:b = move(raw make());
    discard(b.v);
    pass NIL;
};

// THE HOLE THE SHALLOW CHECK HAD. A member access was accepted whatever it was
// built on, so this stored into a field of a value with no home — the outermost
// operator was the right shape and the root was not.
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-024
func:store_into_a_temporary = NIL() never fails {
    (raw make()).v = 5i32;
    pass NIL;
};
